All callback offsets are monotonic; maximum deviation from the continuous smoothstep curve is below 0.001 DIP. Sampled 800 DIP full slides at 60/120/144 Hz have maximum per-frame deltas 71.334/35.692/29.747 DIP, each bounded by the ease curve's maximum slope. Actual callback frequency here is approximately 240 Hz with occasional gaps; this is callback evidence, not a claim about the physical monitor's refresh or scanout.

## Needs the user's eyes

Physical 60/120/144 Hz presentation, wheel/drag settle feel, heading weight/size and palette preference, physical chevron reach, and menu keyboard focus on the desktop. Animation-frame callbacks still execute on the UI thread; this removes timer quantization but does not guarantee smooth presentation while that thread is blocked. Virtual callback timing and deterministic refresh-rate sampling are not monitor/scanout evidence. Scripted show-to-focus is internal, not physical hotkey-to-paint. Card's neighbour clipping during a deliberately frozen mid-drag and existing flush-left code panels remain as before.

## Final confirmation

Final-binary `--selftest`: **exit 0**, `SELFTEST PASS: Quiet + Card production shells and retained Classic capability suite`, followed by `T006 PASS: successful quit start/finish logs`. [Raw selftest log](../../docs/design/m6/built/selftest-T014.log). Exact empty-source/nonempty-preview regression and both themes/shells passed. Release build is 0 warnings / 0 errors. All 40 requested renders plus menu/fold and heading proofs were regenerated and viewed. No remaining scripted-check blocker.

Changed source: `Views/Common/{ShellPalette,ShellLayoutContext,ResidentShellWindow,ResidentShellWindow.Appearance,ResidentShellWindow.Smoke,ShellRender}.cs`, `Views/Classic/ClassicShellWindow.Smoke.cs`, `Editor/{ChevronMargin,AlignmentProof,StrikeProof,ActiveGuideProof}.cs`, `Carousel/CarouselController.cs`. Core/ViewModels: **untouched**. No git operations; manager handles review/commit.
# Evaluation lenses

## 1. Heuristic evaluation (Nielsen, 10 heuristics)
- Rules of thumb, not strict guidelines. Cheap, fast, works on specs, prototypes and finished software.
- Best with 3-5 independent evaluators and a severity scale. A single-evaluator pass is a stated limitation.
- Headings include visibility of system status, consistency and standards, aesthetic and minimalist design, help and documentation.

## 2. Cognitive walkthrough
Per task step ask:
1. Will the user see the action is available?
2. Will they recognise it as the one they need?
3. After acting, will they understand the feedback?
This is the closest method to "feeling" the product.

## 3. WCAG 2.2 (AA unless noted)
- Text contrast 4.5:1 (large text 3:1); non-text UI and focus indicators 3:1.
- Target size minimum 24x24 px (44 px is a stricter house rule for touch).
- 2.4.7 visible focus; 2.4.11 focus not obscured (AA); 2.4.13 focus appearance is **AAA**, not AA.
- 2.5.7 dragging movements need a single-pointer alternative.
- Reduced motion: best practice only (2.3.3 is AAA). Pause/stop for moving content is the AA-level check.
- Automated tools catch only part; add manual keyboard and assistive-tech testing.
- Web criteria; apply to a desktop app by intent, not by letter.

## 4. Windows 11 / Fluent 2
- Principles: functional, natural, consistent, appealing. Larger elements get more motion time; reduced-motion variants expected.
- Mica for app windows, Acrylic for transient surfaces. Official token values were not confirmed; check Microsoft Learn.
- Judge native fit: title bar, snap behaviour, focus, backdrop over light and dark wallpapers, DPI changes.

## 5. Motion (Apple HIG as the feel target)
- Interruptible, retargetable motion; springs carry velocity; feedback on every input; crossfade under Reduce Motion.

## Severity scale
| Level | Meaning |
|---|---|
| S0 | Cosmetic, nice to fix. |
| S1 | Minor friction, workaround obvious. |
| S2 | Real friction or exclusion for some users. |
| S3 | Blocks a task, loses data, or breaks the core promise (speed, summon, type). |

